---
import ConverterPage from '../components/ConverterPage.astro';

const faq = [
  {
    q: 'Is Mangal to DevLys the same as Unicode to DevLys?',
    a: 'Yes. Mangal is a Unicode font, not a separate encoding, so text set in Mangal is already Unicode. This page and <a href="/unicode-to-devlys">Unicode to DevLys</a> run exactly the same conversion - this one exists because people arrive having been told their source is "Mangal".',
  },
  {
    q: 'Why are there two copy buttons?',
    a: 'Because the output box is showing you one thing and holding another. It reads as Hindi only because the DevLys font is drawing ASCII keys. <strong>Copy DevLys code</strong> gives the raw keys for Word; <strong>Copy as Hindi (Unicode)</strong> gives the Unicode back. Both are generated so you never have to guess which one the clipboard took.',
  },
  {
    q: 'The output looks like Latin gibberish. Is it broken?',
    a: 'No - that is what DevLys is. The font turns <code>Hkkjr</code> into <strong lang="hi">भारत</strong>. Use the Preview / Raw toggle above the output to switch how it is displayed; the toggle is purely visual and never changes what gets copied.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Do I need the DevLys font installed?',
    a: 'To use the result in Word, yes: install DevLys 010 and apply it to the pasted text, or press Download .docx and the font name is written into the file for you. Nothing needs installing to see the preview here.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Why would I convert modern Hindi back to a legacy font?',
    a: 'Usually because something downstream has not moved on: a press or publishing workflow built around DevLys, a form whose layout was designed for it, an archive that has to stay consistent, or a typing test that is marked in the legacy layout.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Does my text get uploaded anywhere?',
    a: 'No. Everything runs in your browser; nothing is sent to a server, and the page keeps working offline once loaded.',
  },
];
---

<ConverterPage
  path="/mangal-to-devlys"
  lede="Paste Mangal or any Unicode Hindi on the left and get DevLys 010 font text on the right, ready for legacy documents. Nothing is uploaded."
  faq={faq}
>
  <h2>What this converter does</h2>
  <p>
    If your text is set in Mangal, it is already Unicode - Mangal is a Unicode
    font that ships with Windows, not an encoding of its own. What you need is
    the step <em>after</em> that: turning Unicode Hindi into the ASCII
    keystrokes the DevLys font expects. That is what this page does.
  </p>
  <p>
    The output will look like nonsense Latin text. That is correct and it is the
    whole point: DevLys stores Hindi as ASCII and leaves the drawing to the
    font. Paste it into Word, apply DevLys 010, and it reads as Hindi.
  </p>

  <h2>The reordering, in reverse</h2>
  <p>
    Unicode keeps Hindi in <em>logical</em> order and DevLys keeps it in
    <em>visual</em> order, so this direction has to undo every rearrangement the
    forward one applies:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      The short-i matra moves back in front of its consonant -
      <strong lang="hi">कि</strong> becomes <code>fd</code>.
    </li>
    <li>
      The reph moves back after the cluster it sits on, so
      <strong lang="hi">कर्म</strong> becomes <code>deZ</code>.
    </li>
    <li>
      Composed vowel signs split apart again: <strong lang="hi">को</strong>
      becomes an aa-matra plus an e-matra.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Text converted to DevLys and back arrives unchanged, punctuation included -
    that round trip is part of the test suite rather than something you have to
    take on trust.
  </p>

  <h2>Why this page is live now</h2>
  <p>
    DevLys 010 and Kruti Dev 010 share a keyboard layout, and that overlap was
    verified rather than assumed: every character code this converter can emit
    was read out of both font files and compared. All of them match. The two
    fonts do differ in one block of character codes, and the conversion table
    never uses it - which is precisely why this page could go live while the
    Chanakya ones could not.
  </p>

  <h2>How to use it</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>Paste your Mangal or Unicode Hindi into the left panel.</li>
    <li>Press <strong>Copy DevLys code</strong>.</li>
    <li>
      In Word, paste and apply the <strong>DevLys 010</strong> font - or press
      <strong>Download .docx</strong>, which sets it for you.
    </li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    For the other direction see <a href="/devlys-to-mangal">DevLys to Mangal</a>.
    If the legacy font you actually need is Kruti Dev,
    <a href="/mangal-to-kruti-dev">Mangal to Kruti Dev</a> produces the same
    keystrokes under that name.
  </p>
</ConverterPage>
